Fibromyalgia
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Chronic widespread musculoskeletal pain, Unrefreshing sleep, Somatic hyperalgesia without inflammation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-022
Reason for Visit: Severe, acute-on-chronic pain crisis and profound exhaustion rendering the patient unable to perform basic activities of daily living.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient with an established central sensitization disorder experiencing an acute, unmanageable flare of widespread pain, severe mechanical allodynia, and functional collapse following a minor stressor. Urgent assessment was required to exclude a superimposed acute inflammatory arthropathy or systemic infection, and to implement immediate pharmacological and behavioral strategies to arrest the severe neuro-inflammatory crisis prior to daytime services.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My entire body feels like it's been beaten with a bat; every joint aches, my skin hurts to touch, and I am too exhausted to even sit up."
History of Present Illness: Patient with a known history of Fibromyalgia presents with a severe acute exacerbation. Timeline: Baseline chronic, widespread, dull musculoskeletal pain above and below the waist bilaterally; rapidly worsened over the last 48 hours following a stressful family event. Currently reports heightened pain sensitivity and severe mechanical allodynia to light pressure. Patient describes a profound fatigue and an exhausted state, stating sleep has been entirely unrefreshing for a week. Reports a subjective feeling of joint swelling in the hands and knees, and severe morning stiffness that lasted until 1400 hours today. Severe cognitive fog ("fibro fog") with notable word-finding difficulties during the interview.
Functional Status: Bedbound due to severe fatigue and widespread hyperalgesia; entirely dependent on spouse for basic care today.
Social/Safety Concerns: Caregiver burnout; significant psychological distress secondary to unrelenting pain and functional loss.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 122/78 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.5 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Alert, visibly exhausted, speaking slowly, lying perfectly still in bed to minimize sensory input.
Physical Exam: Widespread hyperalgesia noted; exquisitely tender to light palpation across multiple anatomically defined tender points (occiput, lower cervical, trapezius, supraspinatus, second rib, lateral epicondyle, gluteal, greater trochanter, medial fat pad of knee). Crucially, detailed examination of the hands, wrists, and knees reveals absolutely no objective inflammatory joint effusions, erythema, or warmth, despite the patient's subjective feeling of swelling. Deep tendon reflexes are 2+ and symmetrical. Motor strength is 5/5 globally (though testing is limited by pain inhibition). Sensation to light touch/pinprick is intact globally, but interpreted as painful (allodynia). No lymphadenopathy.
Environmental Observations: Room is darkened; patient wearing earplugs to block out sensory stimuli.
Medication Reconciliation: Baseline Amitriptyline and Pregabalin taken as prescribed. Over-the-counter NSAIDs (Ibuprofen) completely ineffective.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute severe flare of Fibromyalgia (Central Sensitization Crisis) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Rheumatoid Arthritis Flare - Ruled out by the definitive absence of objective clinical synovitis (no boggy swelling, heat, or effusions in the joints) despite the subjective feeling of swelling.

Polymyalgia Rheumatica - Ruled out by the widespread nature of the pain (rather than strictly shoulder/pelvic girdle) and the presence of severe allodynia, though ESR/CRP testing is required.

Systemic Infection (e.g., Viral Myositis) - Ruled out by the absence of fever, rigors, respiratory or gastrointestinal symptoms.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a severe, functionally paralyzing crisis of central pain sensitization. The critical clinical task was to firmly differentiate this massive subjective pain flare from a true objective inflammatory or infectious process. Clinical examination definitively ruled out acute synovitis or systemic infection, confirming that the crisis was driven by a hyper-reactive central nervous system rather than peripheral tissue damage. Postponing evaluation would have subjected the patient to unmanageable distress and severe caregiver strain. Urgent after-hours assessment provided crucial diagnostic validation (ruling out "new" damage), prevented the inappropriate use of high-dose corticosteroids or opioids, and established a safe, supportive plan focusing on central nervous system down-regulation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered low-dose oral Diazepam 2mg STAT to explicitly reduce acute central nervous system hyperarousal and muscle tension. Advised against escalating opioid or NSAID use, as they are ineffective for central sensitization.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Implement a strict "sensory diet" for the next 24 hours: dim lights, silence, and slow, deep diaphragmatic breathing for 5 minutes every hour. Consequence: Direct down-regulation of the sympathetic nervous system, slowly dialling down the "volume" of the amplified pain signals (negative reinforcement).
Action: Apply generalized, gentle heat (e.g., an electric blanket on low) rather than targeted hot packs. Consequence: Providing a broad, soothing, non-noxious stimulus to the skin, which competes with and blocks the painful allodynia signals reaching the brain (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3-5 days to evaluate the flare, check routine inflammatory markers (ESR/CRP to definitively rule out concurrent issues), and adjust baseline neuropathic medications.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if there is a sudden onset of high fever, visibly red and hot swollen joints, or the development of a new, severe, localized pain that is completely different from the baseline widespread ache.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Fibromyalgia flares are characterized by a mismatch between the severity of pain felt and the absence of actual tissue damage. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: This acute crisis will slowly retreat over 3-7 days with aggressive rest and sensory down-regulation. Pushing through the pain during a flare will only prolong the hyper-sensitized state.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if a specific joint suddenly becomes red, hot, and swollen like a balloon, or if you develop a high fever and the shivers.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your brain's pain amplifier is currently stuck on the maximum setting due to recent stress; it is interpreting normal touch and movement as severe tissue damage. Because there is no actual inflammation in your joints, standard anti-inflammatories will not work. We must use sensory rest to calm the brain back down.
Actionable Steps: Stop trying to push through. Your body requires complete sensory quiet. Use the electric blanket on low to provide a safe, soothing feeling to your skin; this helps override the burning, painful signals.

